    "main_topic": [
      {
        "h_title": "4-6 servings",
        "main_title": "4-6 servings",
        "author": null,
        "language": "en",
        "level": 3,
        "primary_content": [
          {
            "text": "Heat the oil in a medium stockpot over medium-low heat.\nAdd the shallots and garlic and fry until fragrant, about 5 minutes. Add\nthe shrimp paste, stirring until it has disintegrated and become fragrant,\nanother 2 minutes. Add the canned mackerel (including the tomato sauce),\nstirring until disintegrated, fragrant, and a thin layer of oil emerges, another\n5 minutes.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Increase the heat slightly and add the tomatoes. Fry, stirring frequently,\nuntil the tomatoes have mostly disintegrated, the mixture has reduced\nslightly, and the oil reemerges, about 20 minutes.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Add the pork and fry, stirring frequently, until the oil reemerges, another\n10 minutes. Add the pork bones and 2 quarts of water, and increase the heat\nto high. When the mixture reaches a simmer, reduce the heat and simmer\nfor 20 minutes. Add the bouillon powder and salt, and simmer another 10 to\n15 minutes.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Taste, adjusting the seasoning if necessary; the broth should be relatively\nthin and should taste tart, savory, and salty (in that order).",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "To each shallow serving bowl, add 3½ ounces of khanom jiin\nnoodles and top with 1 cup of the broth (remove stock bones if they are too big\nto serve). Garnish with crispy garlic and garlic oil, cilantro, and green onion,\nand serve with with shredded cabbage, lime wedges, and small bowls of salt\nand chile powder.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
